    "content": "# Singaporean Buyer's Guide to Property in Malaysia\n\nSingaporeans are the largest group of foreign buyers I work with, and the same three questions come up every time: can I buy while I still own my HDB flat, what does it cost me at the door, and how do I get there. This guide answers those in plain terms. Where a rule or a rate is involved, I say so and tell you who to confirm it with — the rules on both sides of the Causeway change, and I would rather you check than take a number from a web page.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The HDB Minimum Occupation Period comes first\n\nIf you own an HDB flat, the Minimum Occupation Period is the first thing to settle, before you look at a single unit in Malaysia. HDB rules restrict flat owners from acquiring private residential property, in Singapore or overseas, until the MOP on the flat has been served. Breaching it has serious consequences for the flat itself.\n\nCheck your own MOP status on the HDB portal before you sign anything binding here — a booking form counts. If you are unsure whether your flat type or purchase route is caught by the rule, ask HDB directly. I can tell you what Malaysian law requires; only HDB can tell you what your flat allows.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What you pay on the Malaysian side\n\nMalaysia does not levy an additional stamp duty on foreign buyers the way Singapore does. A Singaporean buying here pays the same stamp duty on the transfer as a Malaysian citizen, on a tiered scale set by the Inland Revenue Board, plus legal fees and a nominal duty on the sale and purchase agreement. What Malaysia does apply to foreign buyers is a **minimum purchase price**, set state by state — RM 1 million in Kuala Lumpur, higher in parts of Selangor and Penang, and different again in Johor's designated zones. My [state-by-state guide](/blog/foreigner-buying-property-in-malaysia) walks through those.\n\nOn disposal there is Real Property Gains Tax, charged on the profit and stepped by how long you held the property, with a separate schedule for non-citizens. On rental income there is Malaysian income tax for non-residents, with the usual deductions for interest, management charges and repairs. I do not print the rates here because they are revised in the annual budget; your solicitor or a Malaysian tax agent will give you the current schedule, and the Singapore–Malaysia double taxation agreement is what stops the same income being taxed twice.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Money: CPF, financing and the currency\n\n- **CPF cannot be used** for property outside Singapore. The deposit, duties, fees and instalments come from cash.\n- **Malaysian banks do lend to Singaporeans.** The margin of finance depends on the bank, your income documentation and where that income is earned; it is lower than what a Malaysian resident gets. Get an indication in writing from a bank before you commit, not after.\n- **The currency works both ways.** Borrowing in ringgit and collecting rent in ringgit gives you a natural hedge on the loan. Funding the deposit from Singapore dollars is where the exchange rate helps — or does not, depending on the year you buy.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Getting there: the Causeway, the Second Link and the RTS\n\nFor Johor Bahru, how you cross decides how you live.\n\n- **The Causeway** into JB city centre is the busiest land border crossing in the world, and at peak hours the queue is measured in hours, not minutes.\n- **The Second Link at Tuas** serves the western side — Iskandar Puteri, Medini and the townships around them — and is usually the calmer crossing.\n- **The RTS Link** between Woodlands North and Bukit Chagar is built to take a few minutes station to station, with immigration for both countries cleared once at departure. It is officially scheduled to open at the end of 2026; treat the opening date as the operator's plan until trains are running. My [RTS guide](/blog/rts-johor-bahru-guide) covers which projects sit within reach of the station.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The paperwork that protects you\n\n- **State consent.** Every foreign purchase needs the state authority's consent. Your solicitor applies for it after the agreement is signed; allow months, not weeks.\n- **A Malaysian solicitor of your own.** Not the developer's panel lawyer. They check the title, the developer's housing development account and, on a completed unit, the Certificate of Completion and Compliance.\n- **Tax residency.** Under the Common Reporting Standard, account information moves between the two countries automatically. Declare what you are required to declare on both sides; a tax agent in each country is cheap relative to the alternative.\n\n---\n\nI am Shyan Yee, REN 46305 with IQI Realty in Kuala Lumpur. If you are a Singaporean buyer weighing KL against JB, message me on WhatsApp and I will send you the projects that fit your budget and the state thresholds that apply to them.",
